√ ''vah'', go, move. The orig. sense ‘carry’ passed into that of ‘raise, lift,’ and thence into that of ‘weigh.’ Hence ult. (< AS. ''wegan'', etc.) ''wag''<sup>1</sup>, ''wagon'', ''wain''<sup>1</sup>, ''way''<sup>1</sup>, ''wight''<sup>1</sup>, ''whit'', and (< L. ''vehere'') ''vehicle'', ''convection'', etc.: see esp. ''way''<sup>1</sup>.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To raise or lift; bear up: as, to ''weigh'' anchor; to ''weigh'' a ship that has been sunk.
{{smaller block|And so ye same mornyng we ''wayde'' our ancre and made sayle, and come into the foresayd hauyn at Mylo.
{{right|''Sir R. Guylforde'', Pylgrymage, p. 63.}}
[The ship] struck upon a rock, and, being forced to run ashore to save her men, could never be ''weighed'' since, although she lies a great height above the water.
{{right|''Winthrop'', Hist. New England, I. 3.}}}}
'''2.''' To bear up or balance in order to determine the weight of; determine the relative heaviness of (something) by comparison in a balance with some recognized standard; ascertain the number of pounds, ounces, etc., in: as, to ''weigh'' sugar; to ''weigh'' gold.
{{smaller block|Like stuffe haue I read in S. Francis Legend, of the ballance wherein mens deedes are ''weighed'', and the Deuill lost his prey by the weight of a Chalice.
{{right|''Purchas'', Pilgrimage, p. 140.}}
The hunter took up his rifle instinctively from the corner of the room, ''weighed'' it in both hands held palm upward.
{{right|''W. M. Baker'', New Timothy, p. 297.}}}}
'''3.''' To consider or examine for the purpose of forming an opinion or coming to a conclusion; compare; estimate deliberately and maturely; balance; ponder: as, to ''weigh'' the advantages and disadvantages of a scheme.

readings.c1s2:
{{smaller block|<poem>In noble corage oghte been areste,
And ''weyen'' every thing by equitee.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Good Women, l. 398.}}}}
{{smaller block|Wherefore I pray you ''weigh'' this with yourself the better, and see whether you can espy how your doctrine is doubtful. {{right|''J. Bradford'', Letters (Parker Soc., 1853), II. 130.}}}}
{{smaller block|Regard not who it is which speaketh, but ''weigh'' only what is spoken. {{right|''Hooker'', Eccles. Polity, Pref., i.}}}}
{{smaller block|''Weigh'' oath with oath, and you will nothing ''weigh''. {{right|''Shak.'', M. N. D., iii. 2. 131.}}}}
'''4†.''' To consider as worthy of notice; make account of; care for; regard; esteem.
{{smaller block|You ''weigh'' me not? O, that’s you care not for me. {{right|''Shak.'', L. L. L., v. 2. 27.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>You are light, gentlemen,
Nothing to ''weigh'' your hearts.</poem>
{{right|''Fletcher and Shirley'', Night-Walker, i. 1.}}}}
'''5.''' To overweigh or overpower; burden; oppress. See the following phrase.—'''To weigh down.''' (''a''†) To preponderate over.
{{smaller block|He ''weighs'' King Richard ''down''. {{right|''Shak.'', Rich. II., iii. 4. 89.}}}}
{{smaller|(''b'') To oppress with weight or heaviness; overburden; depress.}}
{{smaller block|Thou [sleep] no more wilt ''weigh'' my eyelids ''down''. {{right|''Shak.'', 2 Hen. IV., iii. 1. 7.}}}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To weigh anchor; get under way or in readiness to sail.
{{smaller block|When he was aboard his bark, he ''weighed'' and set sail, and shot off all his guns. {{right|''Winthrop'', Hist. New England, II. 232.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>The vessel ''weighs'', forsakes the shore,
And lessens to the sight.</poem>
{{right|''Cowper'', The Bird’s Nest.}}}}
'''2.''' To have weight, literally or figuratively.
{{smaller block|Alliances, how near soever, ''weigh'' but light in the Scales}}

readings.c1s3:
